# Which average effect are you even estimating?
subtitle: "Why the average effect for those who sign up is not the effect for everyone, and why a flawless experiment can give the wrong number for the next state."
tags: ["causal inference","treatment effect heterogeneity","external validity","medicaid"]
teaches: ["methods/potential-outcomes","concepts/treatment-effect-heterogeneity","concepts/external-validity"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow"}
next: {"title":"Nobody complies: intention-to-treat versus the effect of treatment","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/nobody-complies-intention-to-treat-versus-the-effect-of-treatment.cast"}

## Three averages, one town
voice: female
level: advanced

    text h_n "People" x 660 y 705 font_size 56 hidden true
    math h_d tex D_i size 64 x 910 y 705 hidden true
    math h_tau tex \tau_i size 66 colors {"\\tau_i":"#8a5fa8"} x 1170 y 705 hidden true
    path rule_h thick #3d3833 points [[50,660],[1300,660]] hidden true
    text r1_name "Chronic ill" x 260 y 605 font_size 64 hidden true
    text r1_n "20" x 660 y 605 font_size 70 hidden true
    text r1_d "1" #2f6b8f x 910 y 605 font_size 72 hidden true
    text r1_tau "8" #8a5fa8 x 1170 y 605 font_size 74 hidden true
    text r2_name "Now and then" x 260 y 517 font_size 64 hidden true
    text r2_n "20" x 660 y 517 font_size 70 hidden true
    text r2_d "1" #2f6b8f x 910 y 517 font_size 72 hidden true
    text r2_tau "4" #8a5fa8 x 1170 y 517 font_size 74 hidden true
    text r3_name "Rarely ill" x 260 y 429 font_size 64 hidden true
    text r3_n "60" x 660 y 429 font_size 70 hidden true
    text r3_d "0" #b5482e x 910 y 429 font_size 72 hidden true
    text r3_tau "1" #8a5fa8 x 1170 y 429 font_size 74 hidden true
    path rule_s #8f887c points [[50,385],[1300,385]] style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    math ate_m tex "\\mathrm{ATE} = E[\\tau_i]" size 66 x 500 y 322 hidden true
    text ate_v "3" #8a5fa8 x 1170 y 322 font_size 72 hidden true
    math att_m tex "{\\mathrm{ATT}} = E[\\tau_i \\mid {D_i = 1}]" size 66 colors {"{D_i = 1}":"#2f6b8f","{\\mathrm{ATT}}":"#2f6b8f"} x 500 y 235 hidden true
    text att_v "6" #8a5fa8 x 1170 y 235 font_size 72 hidden true
    math atu_m tex "{\\mathrm{ATU}} = E[\\tau_i \\mid {D_i = 0}]" size 66 colors {"{D_i = 0}":"#b5482e","{\\mathrm{ATU}}":"#b5482e"} x 500 y 148 hidden true
    text atu_v "1" #8a5fa8 x 1170 y 148 font_size 72 hidden true
    text pi_t "π = 0.4 signed up" #2f6b8f x 1150 y 50 font_size 52 hidden true
    math dec tex "\\mathrm{ATE} = \\pi \\cdot \\mathrm{ATT} + (1-\\pi) \\cdot \\mathrm{ATU}" size 64 x 560 y 50 hidden true
    figure table aspect 9:5 members ["h_n","h_d","h_tau","rule_h","r1_name","r1_n","r1_d","r1_tau","r2_name","r2_n","r2_d","r2_tau","r3_name","r3_n","r3_d","r3_tau","rule_s","ate_m","ate_v","att_m","att_v","atu_m","atu_v","pi_t","dec"] hidden true

    card title "Three averages, one town"

When a study says coverage adds so many good days (@draw h_tau@), whose days is it talking about?
    draw h_n rule_h

It matters, because a state paying for an expansion is buying some (@draw r1_name r2_name r3_name@) particular people's days. So picture a made-up town of a hundred uninsured adults, of three kinds.

Twenty have a chronic illness, and coverage would add eight good days a month for each of them.
    draw r1_n r1_tau

Twenty more fall ill now and then; for them it adds four.
    draw r2_n r2_tau

And sixty are rarely ill, so coverage adds just a single day.
    draw r3_n r3_tau

Nobody can see these effects person by person. We invent them, so we can check which average a study lands on.
    highlight r1_tau r2_tau r3_tau

Over all hundred people, coverage adds three go (@draw ate_v@)od days on average: the average treatment effect, or ATE.
    draw rule_s ate_m

Now let the state offer coverage, and let people choose.
    draw h_d

Those who expect the most from it sign up: the chronically ill and the now-and-then ill.
    draw r1_d r2_d

The rarely ill don't bother.
    draw r3_d

Over the forty who signed up, coverage adds six days. That's the average effect on the treated, the ATT.
    draw att_m att_v

Over the sixty who stayed out, it adds one: the effect on the untreated, the ATU.
    draw atu_m atu_v

And the overall three is just these two, each weighted by its share of the town.
    draw dec

Call the share who signed up pi: here it is four tenths.
    draw pi_t

Forty percent times six, plus sixty percent times one, makes three.
    morph dec tex "3 = 0.4 \\times 6 + 0.6 \\times 1"

If coverage added three days to everyone, all three averages would be three. They come apart because the gains differ, and people choose by their gains.
    highlight r1_tau r2_tau r3_tau r1_d r2_d r3_d

    quiz "After the voluntary sign-up, the state auto-enrols everyone still uninsured. How many good days does that buy per newly covered person?" intro "A policy to price."
        right: One day: auto-enrolment reaches only the sixty who stayed out, and for them coverage adds one.
        wrong: Who is still uninsured once the eager have signed up?
        * Six: the ATT
        * Three: the ATE
        + One: the ATU

So a policymaker needs no fixed one of these. She needs the average over the people her own policy will add.
    highlight atu_m atu_v

Which raises an awkward question: whose average does a careful experiment hand her?
    highlight att_m att_v

## A fair lottery, a narrow answer
voice: female
level: advanced
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation: y = 1 + 5*x/100
    params:
      p: {value: 40, min: 0, max: 100, step: 5, label: eager share}
    x_range:
      - 0
      - 100
    y_range:
      - 0
      - 7
    show_equation: false
    drag: false
    x_label: ' '
    y_label: ' '
    box:
      x: 100
      'y': 175
      w: 820
      h: 470
    marks:
      - {kind: point, at: p, guides: false}
      - {kind: point, at: 100, id: m_att, guides: false}
      - {kind: point, at: 0, id: m_atu, guides: false}
      - {kind: point, at: 40, id: m_ate, guides: false}
      - {kind: point, at: 20, id: m_new, guides: false}
sources:
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein, Taubman, Wright, Bernstein, Gruber, Newhouse, Allen, Baicker and the Oregon Health Study Group (Quarterly Journal of Economics)
      year: 2012
      finding: In 2008 Oregon drew about 30,000 names by lottery from a waiting list of about 90,000 for roughly 10,000 Medicaid places; winning raised Medicaid coverage by roughly 25 percentage points (income at or below the poverty line and assets under $2,000 were required to enrol).
    - id: kowalski2023
      title: Reconciling Seemingly Contradictory Results from the Oregon Health Insurance Experiment and the Massachusetts Health Reform
      authors: Amanda E. Kowalski (Review of Economics and Statistics; NBER Working Paper 24647)
      year: 2023
      finding: 'In her Oregon analysis sample (single-entrant households, 19,643 people), 41% of lottery winners and 15% of losers were covered: a first stage of 0.26.'

    text l_att "ATT = 6" #2f6b8f at.data [88,6.6] font_size 40 hidden true
    text l_atu "ATU = 1" #b5482e at.data [9,0.45] font_size 40 hidden true
    text l_ate "ATE = 3" #8a5fa8 at.data [28,3.75] font_size 40 hidden true
    text l_new "new state: 2" #3d3833 at.data [31,1.35] font_size 36 hidden true
    text valid "fair draw" #2f6b8f at.data [91,4.55] font_size 38 hidden true
    text readout "{eq.point_y:1} days" #8a5fa8 at.data [70,2] font_size 44 hidden true
    text xt "eager share of the newly covered (%)" #3d3833 at.data [55,-1.15] font_size 34 hidden true
    text yt "good days added" #3d3833 at.data [14,7.6] font_size 34 hidden true
    scratch orc at.percent [77,39] cites ["finkelstein2012","kowalski2023"] work ["Oregon 2008: ~10,000 places","~90,000 signed up","draw moved ~1 in 4"] font_size 28 hidden true
    text xk0 "0" #8f887c at.data [0,-0.45] font_size 32 hidden true
    text xk20 "20" #8f887c at.data [20,-0.45] font_size 32 hidden true
    text xk40 "40" #8f887c at.data [40,-0.45] font_size 32 hidden true
    text xk60 "60" #8f887c at.data [60,-0.45] font_size 32 hidden true
    text xk80 "80" #8f887c at.data [80,-0.45] font_size 32 hidden true
    text xk100 "100" #8f887c at.data [100,-0.45] font_size 32 hidden true
    text yk2 "2" #8f887c at.data [-4,2] font_size 32 hidden true
    text yk4 "4" #8f887c at.data [-4,4] font_size 32 hidden true
    text yk6 "6" #8f887c at.data [-4,6] font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "A fair lottery, a narrow answer"

    hide x_ticks y_ticks x_label y_label

An experiment can be perfectly fair, and still answer a narrower question than the one a state is asking.
    draw axes xk0 xk20 xk40 xk60 xk80 xk100 yk2 yk4 yk6

Across: the s (@draw xt@)hare of the newly covered who are the eager kind, who would sign up on their own. Up: go (@draw yt@)od days added per person.

If everyone covered is eager, the average gain is the ATT from our town: six days.
    draw m_att l_att

If everyone covered is reluctant, it's the ATU: one day.
    draw m_atu l_atu

Every mix in between sits on the straight line joining them: the same weighted average, now as a picture.
    draw curve_0

Our whole town, forty percent eager, lands at three: the ATE.
    draw m_ate l_ate

Now borrow Oregon's design. In 2008 the state had m (@draw orc_box orc_line_1@)oney for about ten thousand more Medicaid places, and about ninety thousand  (@draw orc_line_2@)people put their names down for a lottery.

Only people who signed up could be drawn, and in our town those are the eager forty.
    highlight m_att l_att

Winners and losers come from that same pool, so the draw makes them alike, and the comparison is internally valid.
    draw valid

Run in our town, it would report six days, and six is exactly right, for the volunteers.
    highlight m_att l_att valid

In the real Oregon, only about a quarter more of the winners than the losers ended up covered. So its estimate speaks for a narrower gr (@highlight m_att l_att@)oup still: the people the draw itself moved into coverage.
    draw orc_line_3

    erase orc

    quiz "A neighbouring state will cover all its uninsured, and four in five of them are the reluctant kind. About how many good days per person should it expect?" intro "A new state."
        right: About two: one fifth eager and four fifths reluctant puts it a fifth of the way along the line, at two days.
        wrong: Find the state on the line: how far along is a mix that is one fifth eager?
        * About six
        + About two
        * About four

At one fifth eager the line gives two days: a third of what the lottery f (@highlight m_new m_att@)ound, with the same insurance and the same kinds of people.
    draw m_new l_new

    draw point readout

Now move the point along the line to other states' mixes, and watch the gain per person change with it.
    explore params ["p"] watch "Watch the point slide along the line as the mix changes, and the gain per person with it."

It climbs from one day toward six as the mix gets more eager. The lottery's six holds only at the far right, where everyone is eager.
    animate params.p.value 90 duration 3

Nothing was wrong with the lottery. Its estimate is right for the people the draw moved into coverage; the next state simply covers different people.
    highlight valid

And this line assumes each kind gains the same everywhere. A sicker state, or one with fewer doctors, would move the line itself.
    highlight curve_0

Is this only a toy? Two real reforms suggest it isn't.
    highlight m_new m_att m_atu

## Oregon up, Massachusetts down
voice: female
level: advanced
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation: y = 0.6 - 0.012*x
    x_range:
      - 0
      - 100
    y_range:
      - -0.7
      - 0.8
    show_equation: false
    drag: false
    x_label: ' '
    y_label: ' '
    box:
      x: 100
      'y': 190
      w: 820
      h: 450
sources:
    - id: taubman2014
      title: 'Medicaid Increases Emergency-Department Use: Evidence from Oregon''s Health Insurance Experiment'
      authors: Taubman, Allen, Wright, Baicker and Finkelstein (Science 343:263-268)
      year: 2014
      finding: Using records from twelve Portland-area hospitals for about 25,000 lottery participants over about 18 months, Medicaid coverage increased emergency-department visits by 0.41 per person (SE 0.12; P < 0.001), about 40 percent over a control mean of 1.02.
    - id: kowalski2023
      title: Reconciling Seemingly Contradictory Results from the Oregon Health Insurance Experiment and the Massachusetts Health Reform
      authors: Amanda E. Kowalski (Review of Economics and Statistics 105(3):646-664; NBER Working Paper 24647)
      year: 2023
      finding: In her Oregon sample (19,643 single-entrant individuals) 15% of losers and 41% of winners were covered, and the LATE on ER visits is 0.27, positive but not statistically significant. Compliers were adversely selected relative to never takers (35% vs 31% had a previous ER visit). Under a linearity assumption the effect falls from always takers to compliers to never takers and is negative for never takers. Massachusetts, whose mandate carried a penalty, expanded coverage from 89% to 94% to healthier compliers, comparable to a subset of Oregon never takers; reweighting on common observables cannot reconcile the results, and the predicted Massachusetts decrease is not statistically significant.
takeaways:
    - An average belongs to a population
    - 'Gains vary, people sort: ATT ≠ ATE'
    - Internally valid ≠ valid elsewhere

    polygon b_or thin #2f6b8f points [[15,-0.68],[41,-0.68],[41,0.78],[15,0.78]] data true style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.25 hidden true
    polygon b_ma thin #b5482e points [[89,-0.68],[94,-0.68],[94,0.78],[89,0.78]] data true style.fill #b5482e style.opacity 0.25 hidden true
    text m_or "Oregon: +40%" #2f6b8f at.data [28,0.55] cites ["taubman2014"] font_size 38 hidden true
    text m_ma "Mass.: down or flat" #b5482e at.data [74,-0.42] cites ["kowalski2023"] font_size 34 hidden true
    text e15 "15" #2f6b8f at.data [15,-0.1] font_size 32 hidden true
    text e41 "41" #2f6b8f at.data [41,-0.1] font_size 32 hidden true
    text t_ma2 "89 → 94%" #b5482e at.data [80,0.62] cites ["kowalski2023"] font_size 34 hidden true
    text t_up "↑ more" #8f887c at.data [7,0.3] font_size 34 hidden true
    text t_dn "↓ fewer" #8f887c at.data [7,-0.3] font_size 34 hidden true
    text t_line "her line: shape only" #8a5fa8 at.data [68,0.3] font_size 34 hidden true
    text t_027 "her sample: 0.27, n.s." #2f6b8f at.data [28,-0.5] cites ["kowalski2023"] font_size 30 hidden true
    text xt "covered, most eager first (%)" #3d3833 at.data [52,-0.92] font_size 34 hidden true
    text yt "effect on ER visits" #3d3833 at.data [14,0.92] font_size 34 hidden true
    text xk0 "0" #8f887c at.data [-3,-0.1] font_size 32 hidden true
    text xk50 "50" #8f887c at.data [50,-0.1] font_size 32 hidden true
    text xk100 "100" #8f887c at.data [100,-0.1] font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "Oregon up, Massachusetts down"

    hide x_ticks y_ticks x_label y_label

In Oregon, coverage sent people to the (@draw m_or@) emergency room more often, not less.
    draw axes xk0 xk50 xk100 t_up t_dn

Taubman and colleagues, in Science in 2014, read Portland-area emergency-room records: about eighteen months on, coverage added about four tenths of a visit a person, some forty percent, give or take a quarter of a visit.
    highlight m_or

In Massachusetts, after its 2006 reform pushed coverage close to universal, emergency visits fell, or held steady.
    draw m_ma

One result came from a lottery, the other from a reform with no coin, so it was tempting to trust Oregon alone.
    highlight m_or

    erase m_or m_ma

The economist Amanda Kowalski asked whether the two were even measuring the same people's effect.
    draw yt

Line people up by how readily they take up coverage: the most eager on the left, the most reluctant on the right.
    draw xt

The people Oregon's lottery moved sit toward the left: in her sample, fifteen percent of the losers and forty-one percent of the winners were covered.
    draw b_or e15 e41

And they were a little more likely to have used the emergency room before than those who stayed out even when drawn.
    highlight b_or

Under her assumptions, the effect falls from left to right: positive for the pe (@draw curve_0 t_line ends@)ople the lottery moved, below zero for the reluctant.

Massachusetts started with about nine in ten already insured and moved to about ninety-four, so the people it added were the last, healthier holdouts.
    draw b_ma t_ma2

On her line they sit where the effect is below zero: visits could fall there and rise in Oregon, with both studies right.
    point at.data [91.5,-0.5] gesture circle duration 5

And reweighting Oregon by age, income, or anything else we can measure does not close the gap. The difference runs along eagerness itself.
    highlight b_or b_ma

Not everyone is convinced. Her line assumes the effect changes in a straight line, and in her own sample, people who entered the (@draw t_027@) lottery alone, Oregon's estimate is only about a quarter of a visit, and not statistically significant.
    highlight t_line

She notes that Massachusetts added higher-income people, many enrolling to avoid a penalty. Sceptics say such differences, or differences in the supply of care, could explain the gap on their own.
    highlight t_ma2

A lottery that also reached the reluctant would tell these readings apart.
    highlight b_or b_ma

    quiz "A job-training scheme was tested by lottery among unemployed people who applied for it, because they expected it to help. Now it becomes compulsory for everyone unemployed. Per person, what should the government expect?" intro "One new case."
        right: Smaller: the applicants chose it because they expected to gain; compulsion adds the people who did not, so the trial's fair estimate belongs to a different group.
        wrong: Who applied for the trial, and who does compulsion add?
        * The same effect: the trial was randomized
        + A smaller effect
        * A larger effect: more people take part

    erase t_up t_dn t_027 t_line e15 e41 xk0 xk50 xk100

    animate {"box":{"x":540,"y":250,"w":440,"h":360}} duration 1.5

    move takeaway_1 takeaway_2 takeaway_3 by [-200,0] scale 0.78 duration 0.1

So an average effect is never just the effect of a treatment. It belongs to a population.
    draw takeaway_1

The effect on the treated and the effect on everyone part when gains differ and people choose by them.
    draw takeaway_2

And a perfectly fair experiment is right for the people it moved, and silent about the rest.
    draw takeaway_3

Which average effect are you estimating? The one for the (@highlight b_or b_ma@) people your design happened to move. Before you quote it to another state, ask whether those are the people its policy will move.

